From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Subject: [PATCH] serial: qcom-geni: force runtime suspend across system sleep

A GENI UART held open by its serdev driver (the AYN gamepad MCU on
89c000.serial) keeps its runtime-PM usage count up, so the system sleep
path never reaches the runtime-suspend callback. Its clocks and its
qup-core/qup-config interconnect votes (QUP2, SH1, CN0, CN1, tagged
ALWAYS) then stay in the RPMh sleep set through s2idle, which keeps XO
and the config NoC alive.

Force the runtime suspend after uart_suspend_port() and resume before
uart_resume_port(), as in Praveen Talari's upstream proposal, on top of
the IRQ masking from 1006.

Link: https://ratatoskr.run/linux-arm-msm/2026/07/17196799/t
---
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -1983,6 +1983,7 @@
 	struct qcom_geni_serial_port *port = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
+	int ret;
 
 	/*
 	 * A peer that keeps streaming into a non-console geni UART across the
@@ -2009,7 +2010,18 @@
 		geni_icc_set_tag(&port->se, QCOM_ICC_TAG_ACTIVE_ONLY);
 		geni_icc_set_bw(&port->se);
 	}
-	return uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+	ret = uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (uart_console(uport) && !uport->suspended)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * A port held open keeps its runtime-PM usage count up, so its clocks
+	 * and interconnect votes would otherwise stay on through system sleep.
+	 */
+	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
 }
 
 static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
@@ -2019,6 +2031,10 @@
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
 
+	ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	ret = uart_resume_port(private_data->drv, uport);
 	/* Balance the disable_irq() taken in qcom_geni_serial_suspend(). */
 	if (!uart_console(uport))
